% VERIFIED against Crossref (DOI 10.1007/BF02295996): Psychometrika, vol. 12,
% no. 2, pp. 153-157, June 1947.
% NOTE on faithful use --- CONFIRMED FROM THE SOURCE 2026-08-30 (the 2026-08-23
% version of this note rested on the standard characterisation; the paper has now
% been read, sources/mcnemar1947.pdf, and it holds):
% McNemar presents ONLY the chi-square form. His abstract: "Two formulas are
% presented for judging the significance of the difference between correlated
% proportions. The chi square equivalent of one of the developed formulas is
% pointed out." His eq. (6) is chi^2 = (D-A)^2/(D+A) on the discordant pairs.
% There is NO exact or binomial test anywhere in the paper. The test actually run
% here is the EXACT (binomial) variant --- scipy.stats.binomtest, see
% requirements.txt --- a later extension, not McNemar's own. So the evaluation
% says "an exact (binomial) variant of the McNemar test" and the tables "the
% exact McNemar-type p"; do not write "the McNemar exact test [mcnemar1947]".
% Citing him for the discordant-pair construction (as the methodology does) is
% correct.
% USEFUL EXTRA, now sourced: McNemar's closing sentence limits his own test ---
% "formulas (6) and (5) should not be used unless A + D is 10 or greater." The
% discordant totals here are A+D = 7 (comment-stripping), 8 (comment-preserving)
% and 1 (guarded), all below that bound, so the exact binomial test is not a
% stylistic preference but the methodologically required choice. Available if the
% multiplicity/power discussion in limitations.tex is ever challenged.
@article{mcnemar1947,
  author  = {McNemar, Quinn},
  title   = {Note on the sampling error of the difference between correlated proportions or percentages},
  journal = {Psychometrika},
  volume  = {12},
  number  = {2},
  pages   = {153--157},
  year    = {1947},
  doi     = {10.1007/BF02295996}
}

