% Holm's step-down procedure. NOT in Crossref --- Scand. J. Statist. predates DOI
% coverage for this volume; the paper is on JSTOR (stable/4615733).
% VERIFIED FROM THE SOURCE 2026-08-30: sources/holm1979.pdf title page reads
% "Scandinavian Journal of Statistics, 1979, Vol. 6, No. 2, pp. 65-70", stable
% URL jstor.org/stable/4615733. Every field below is confirmed, including the
% issue number, which OpenAlex leaves null. The earlier "could not be
% machine-verified" caveat is retired.
% DO NOT ADD A DOI. 10.2307/4615733 is NOT registered: the Handle System returns
% responseCode 100 (handle not found) and doi.org / Crossref / DataCite all 404.
% OpenAlex reports it, but that is a constructed JSTOR-pattern identifier, not a
% registered DOI. Adding it would put a dead DOI in the thesis and would fail
% scripts/check-citations.sh. Checked 2026-08-23.
% CLOSED 2026-08-06: the multiplicity discussion in evaluation.tex no longer
% asserts formal "positive dependence" (a technical term in the multiple-testing
% literature, with definitions this design cannot be shown to satisfy). It now
% derives the conservativeness from the Bonferroni bound itself --- Boole's
% inequality is tight only for mutually exclusive rejection events, so correlated
% tests make it loose --- which follows deductively from dunn1961multiple and
% needs no separate source. Do not reintroduce the phrase "positively dependent"
% without a source that defines the notion and shows these tests satisfy it.
@article{holm1979,
  author  = {Holm, Sture},
  title   = {A simple sequentially rejective multiple test procedure},
  journal = {Scandinavian Journal of Statistics},
  volume  = {6},
  number  = {2},
  pages   = {65--70},
  year    = {1979}
}


% Cyclomatic complexity, original source. VERIFIED against Crossref
% (DOI 10.1109/TSE.1976.233837) AND read in full:
% sources/mccabe1976complexity.pdf. Also cited by Heitlager et al. as [12] and by
% Campbell as [3], so both corroborate the bibliographic details.
% Theorem 1: "In a strongly connected graph G, the cyclomatic number is equal to
% the maximum number of linearly independent circuits", and such a set "forms a
% basis for the set of all circuits in G", so any path is a linear combination of
% them. The thesis's "counts the independent paths through a unit of code" is the
% standard gloss on this; strictly it is the SIZE OF A BASIS of independent
% circuits, not a count of all paths. Do not tighten to "all paths".
@article{mccabe1976complexity,
  author  = {McCabe, Thomas J.},
  title   = {A complexity measure},
  journal = {IEEE Transactions on Software Engineering},
  volume  = {SE-2},
  number  = {4},
  pages   = {308--320},
  year    = {1976},
  doi     = {10.1109/TSE.1976.233837}
}
